pthread-embedded bug: pthread_init() (platform/psp/../../pthread_init.c,
shared by every pthread-embedded port, not PSP-specific code) guards its
one-time setup with a plain "if (pte_processInitialized) return; ...;
pte_processInitialized = PTE_TRUE; pte_osInit(); ..." -- a check-then-act
with no lock or atomic protecting either the flag or the pte_osInit()/key/
lock setup that follows it. On a target where more than one native OS
thread can reach the first-ever pthread/libc call before that flag is
set, two threads can both see it false, both proceed, and both run
pte_osInit() (which mallocs and assigns pspsdk's global TLS key-usage
table, libpthreadglue/tls-helper.c's pteTlsGlobalInit/__pteTlsAlloc)
concurrently -- corrupting that global state.

Found while investigating a real boot-time hang (interactive PPSSPP) /
crash (real hardware, ARK CFW) in this project's own PSP EBOOT: this
port's main() calls setup_callbacks() -- which spawns a second PSP
kernel thread via a raw sceKernelCreateThread -- as the very first thing
it does, so from the start of main() there are two live threads free to
race whichever of them first touches pthread/libc machinery. See
docs/adr/0047-psp-memory-budget.md's bug 12 for the full investigation
(guest PCs captured via a diagnostic PPSSPP build resolved cleanly
against this project's own EBOOT to malloc/free internals,
pthread_mutex_init/lock, libgcc's .eh_frame registration, and a tight
loop entirely inside __pteTlsAlloc reading nothing but garbage -- all
consistent with one corrupted pointer, not four independent bugs).

app/psp/main.cxx's own prime_pthread_init() works around this from the
application side (forces pthread_init() to run to completion,
single-threaded, before any other thread exists) but does not fix the
library's own race, and does not help a second, later thread creation
that this project's own code never explicitly calls (most likely
triggered from inside pspsdk/newlib's own lazily-initialized state, not
yet root-caused as of this patch).

Fix: makes the *claim* on the one-time init atomic
(__sync_bool_compare_and_swap on a separate, file-local
pte_processInitState) and makes every other caller wait for the winner
to actually finish instead of skipping the check and racing ahead. Since
this file's own build stage (psptoolchain-allegrex's
scripts/004-pthread-embedded.sh) runs before pspsdk itself, pspthreadman.h
is not on the include path here; sceKernelDelayThread is declared
directly instead, matching pspsdk's own psp/sdk/include/pspthreadman.h.

Not yet upstreamed to https://github.com/pspdev/pthread-embedded --
applies against the "platform_agnostic" branch (confirmed at commit
97fe4ce006b420894f2bcaeb530d1f1f53111fc2, 2026-05-12; the branch this
project's own psptoolchain-allegrex config
(PSPTOOLCHAIN_ALLEGREX_PTHREAD_EMBEDDED_DEFAULT_REPO_REF) pins). Applied
manually, not wired into any automated build in this repo -- unlike
nix/patches/*.patch (which flake.nix applies to PPSSPP automatically),
this project does not manage the PSP cross-toolchain itself, only
consumes a pre-built one, so there is nothing here for this patch to
attach to. Rebuilding and reinstalling it requires only pthread-embedded's
own isolated build stage (its platform/psp/Makefile), not a full
psptoolchain rebuild:

    git clone -b platform_agnostic https://github.com/pspdev/pthread-embedded.git
    cd pthread-embedded
    patch -p1 < /path/to/this/patch
    cd platform/psp
    make clean && make all && make install   # installs into $PSPDEV

Safe to drop once either upstream fixes the same race, or this patch
fails to apply against a future pthread-embedded revision (check
pthread_init.c's own check-then-act for whether the fix already landed
before just bumping context lines).

--- a/pthread_init.c
+++ b/pthread_init.c
@@ -46,9 +46,35 @@

 #include "implement.h"

+/* pspthreadman.h isn't on the include path at this build stage --
+ * pthread-embedded builds before pspsdk in psptoolchain-allegrex's own
+ * bootstrap order, so this project can't depend on its headers. Declared
+ * directly instead; matches pspsdk's own psp/sdk/include/pspthreadman.h. */
+extern int sceKernelDelayThread(unsigned int delay);
+
+/*
+ * pte_processInitialized used to be both the "already done" check and the
+ * "claim the right to do it" flag, read and written with no synchronisation
+ * at all -- on a target where more than one native OS thread can reach
+ * pthread_init() before the first caller finishes (e.g. because it spawns
+ * its own thread as one of its very first actions, before anything else
+ * has necessarily gone through this path even once), two threads can both
+ * see it false, both proceed, and both run pte_osInit() and the key/lock
+ * setup below concurrently -- corrupting whatever global state that setup
+ * touches (pspsdk's TLS key-usage table, in the reported case).
+ *
+ * pte_processInitState makes the *claim* atomic (only one caller's
+ * __sync_bool_compare_and_swap succeeds) and makes every other caller wait
+ * for the winner to actually finish, rather than just skip the check.
+ * pte_processInitialized itself keeps its previous meaning and is still
+ * set exactly once, after initialization is complete, so pte_detach.c and
+ * pthread_terminate.c's own (already fine, since they only ever read after
+ * the fact) checks are unaffected.
+ */
+static volatile int pte_processInitState = 0; /* 0=untouched, 1=claimed, 2=done */
+
 int pthread_init(void)
 {
-
   if (pte_processInitialized)
     {
       /*
@@ -61,7 +87,16 @@ int pthread_init(void)
       return PTE_TRUE;
     }

-  pte_processInitialized = PTE_TRUE;
+  if (!__sync_bool_compare_and_swap(&pte_processInitState, 0, 1))
+    {
+      /* Another thread already claimed initialization; wait for it to
+       * actually finish instead of racing it. */
+      while (pte_processInitState != 2)
+        {
+          sceKernelDelayThread(1000);
+        }
+      return (pte_processInitialized);
+    }

   // Must happen before creating keys.
   pte_osInit();
@@ -85,6 +120,8 @@ int pthread_init(void)
   pte_osMutexCreate (&pte_rwlock_test_init_lock);
   pte_osMutexCreate (&pte_spinlock_test_init_lock);

+  pte_processInitialized = PTE_TRUE;
+  pte_processInitState = 2;

   return (pte_processInitialized);
